    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The hydraulic properties of complex fracture-rock systems can be replaced by \"effective\" properties if the scale of heterogeneity is either much smaller or much larger than the volume (in 3-D) or area (in 2-D) of interest. The effective hydraulic conductivity ellipse for a 2-D representation of fractured rock can be determined through a procedure that involves modeling flow through the rock mass while rotating the direction of hydraulic gradient with respect to the fracture system. For the ideal case of two well-developed, intersecting fracture sets, the major and minor axes of the hydraulic conductivity ellipse can be calculated directly from the fracture aperture, fracture spacing, and the angle between the two fracture sets. An example from a mapped outcrop reveals that one can not evaluate which fractures are hydraulically significant without first knowing the direction of the regional head gradient.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
